{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"THE FIX WORKED, and it proved itself within one hour of merging. Recording the first honest drift\nreport this workflow has produced in days.\n\n## Before and after, from the run record\n\nChange 2658 merged as `1f1ecea07307`. The very next scheduled Terraform Drift run\n(`33950205876`, 06:34 UTC):\n\n    job \"fmt\"   -> SUCCESS\n    job \"plan\"  -> FAILURE at step \"terraform plan (fail on drift)\", exit code 2\n\nCompare the three runs before the fix: the whole run died in **22-29 seconds** at `fmt -check`\nand `plan` NEVER EXECUTED. Now `fmt` passes in its own job and `plan` actually runs — which is the\nentire point of splitting them, and the reason the exit code is now a drift verdict rather than a\nformatting complaint.\n\n## What it found\n\n    aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.var_tmp_cleanup_not_active   will be created\n    aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.var_tmp_usage_high           will be created\n    aws_instance.gerrit                                      will be updated in-place\n        user_data  73abb917c5322e67... -> c8bc65ae948d1a...\n\nThese are **story S4's own alarms** (`2ba3-bf77`, \"Bound /var/tmp growth and alarm on tmp\nsaturation\"), which merged roughly an hour before this run and have not been applied.\n\n## Why this is the exact failure this ticket was filed for\n\nThis ticket exists because story S1 declared a dedicated gate-scratch EBS volume, its attachment\nand two alarms — and **none of them were ever applied**, and nobody knew for two days, because\n`fmt -check` was aborting the run before `plan` could say so. That is now tracked separately as\n`renowned-corked-hapuku` (`dcc3-75ee-26ce-4840`), where the volume is still attached-but-raw.\n\nS4 has just reproduced the same shape: declared alarms, not applied. The difference is that this\ntime the workflow said so **within the hour**, unprompted, instead of the condition accumulating\nsilently. That is the whole value of the change and it is now demonstrated on live infrastructure\nrather than argued from a test.\n\n## Action arising, deliberately NOT taken in this comment\n\nThe two alarms are non-disruptive to create. The `aws_instance.gerrit` update is not: changing\n`user_data` on a running EC2 instance requires a STOP/START, and the plan shows `public_ip` and\n`public_dns` going to `(known after apply)`. So an unscoped apply would take Gerrit down and\npotentially move its address. Noting the split rather than acting on it here; any apply should be\nscoped, and the instance half is an operator-timed decision, not a drift-remediation reflex.\n\nAlso worth stating plainly: `user_data` only takes effect at boot, so applying that half changes\nnothing operationally until the instance is next started — the stop/start IS the change, not a\nside effect of it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788591053437907001,"uuid":"de4e535c-0011-48e4-a5d2-a154861f02a3"}